1
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:02,280
Europe can still win the race

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,980
for artificial intelligence.

3
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:06,170
Everyone says it has already lost.

4
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:07,190
Too slow.

5
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,210
Too small.

6
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:09,360
Too many rules.

7
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:11,040
And whoever builds this technology

8
00:00:11,080 --> 00:00:12,770
gets a say in how it treats you.

9
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:14,810
Which AI reads your contracts.

10
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:16,350
Sits in on your job interview.

11
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:18,320
Says yes or no to your loan.

12
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:19,360
Wherever you live.

13
00:00:19,990 --> 00:00:21,310
Right now, almost none

14
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:23,110
of that AI is European.

15
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:24,200
So how could Europe

16
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,730
possibly still win?

17
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:26,950
Guess how much

18
00:00:26,990 --> 00:00:27,970
of the world's computing

19
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:29,230
power for AI sits

20
00:00:29,270 --> 00:00:30,550
in the European Union.

21
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:32,310
About 5%.

22
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:34,870
And yet: the only machines

23
00:00:34,910 --> 00:00:36,080
on Earth that can print

24
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:37,650
the most advanced chips

25
00:00:37,690 --> 00:00:38,920
are built in Europe.

26
00:00:39,050 --> 00:00:40,370
Then shipped away.

27
00:00:40,630 --> 00:00:42,580
Many of the people who build AI

28
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:43,960
are trained in Europe.

29
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:45,440
Then hired away.

30
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:46,830
And the European Union

31
00:00:46,870 --> 00:00:47,860
has more people

32
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:49,460
than the United States.

33
00:00:49,510 --> 00:00:51,830
So Europe isn't too small.

34
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:54,020
It's in 27 pieces.

35
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:58,260
27 countries, 27 sets of rules.

36
00:00:58,430 --> 00:00:59,320
And the plan to put them

37
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:01,660
together is already on the table.

38
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:03,340
This winter, the first

39
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:04,910
big decisions are due.

40
00:01:05,520 --> 00:01:06,910
Those chip machines come

41
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:08,430
from a single company,

42
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:09,510
in a Dutch town.

43
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,800
Each is the size of a bus.

44
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:14,520
Shipping one fills 3 cargo jets.

45
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:16,620
And almost every advanced AI chip

46
00:01:16,660 --> 00:01:18,670
in the world was printed by one.

47
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:19,660
This year,

48
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:21,350
by its own executive's account,

49
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:22,450
the company has sold

50
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:24,530
absolutely nothing in Europe.

51
00:01:24,780 --> 00:01:26,200
So the strengths are real.

52
00:01:26,290 --> 00:01:27,590
They're just not connected.

53
00:01:28,020 --> 00:01:29,080
The plan to connect them

54
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:30,120
isn't new.

55
00:01:30,190 --> 00:01:32,030
Mario Draghi, the man who once

56
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:34,040
saved the euro, wrote it down.

57
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:34,960
A year later,

58
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,630
he gave his own verdict:

59
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:38,770
One year on,

60
00:01:38,810 --> 00:01:40,670
Europe is therefore

61
00:01:40,710 --> 00:01:43,550
in a harder place.

62
00:01:43,590 --> 00:01:45,600
Our growth model is fading.

63
00:01:46,070 --> 00:01:47,460
A harder place.

64
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:49,660
And today, by one count,

65
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:52,130
only about 1 in 6 of his proposals

66
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:53,840
has been fully carried out.

67
00:01:54,210 --> 00:01:55,870
For AI, five parts

68
00:01:55,910 --> 00:01:57,490
of the plan matter most.

69
00:01:57,690 --> 00:01:58,480
Money.

70
00:01:58,520 --> 00:01:59,940
One set of rules.

71
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:00,920
Energy.

72
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:01,910
A lab.

73
00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:03,110
And minds.

74
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:05,210
That last one can't be built.

75
00:02:05,270 --> 00:02:06,550
It has to be won.

76
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:08,720
One test for all five:

77
00:02:08,910 --> 00:02:10,160
is it really there,

78
00:02:10,200 --> 00:02:11,450
or only on paper?

79
00:02:12,630 --> 00:02:13,760
Money first.

80
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:15,660
Europe isn't short of it.

81
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:16,980
In the European Union,

82
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:17,820
households keep

83
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:19,650
about 10 trillion euros

84
00:02:19,690 --> 00:02:20,770
in bank accounts.

85
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:22,010
If you live there,

86
00:02:22,050 --> 00:02:23,350
part of that is yours.

87
00:02:23,550 --> 00:02:25,300
A young AI company in Paris

88
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:27,490
or Munich barely sees any of it.

89
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,100
So its founders fly to California.

90
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:31,050
The plan isn't

91
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:32,710
to take anyone's savings.

92
00:02:32,830 --> 00:02:33,930
It's to make investing them

93
00:02:33,970 --> 00:02:35,310
in Europe easier.

94
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:37,340
Most of it isn't law yet.

95
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:38,500
Critics warn:

96
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:39,950
push savers into markets,

97
00:02:39,990 --> 00:02:41,560
and you push them into risk.

98
00:02:41,820 --> 00:02:42,530
But the money going

99
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:43,710
into Europe's start-ups

100
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:45,900
is up by more than half this year.

101
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:48,400
Europe now has its own fund,

102
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:49,930
so its biggest start-ups

103
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:51,490
can grow without leaving.

104
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:54,290
One of its first bets: Mistral,

105
00:02:54,330 --> 00:02:56,640
Europe's best-known AI company.

106
00:02:56,930 --> 00:02:57,940
A year earlier,

107
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:59,300
Mistral's lead investor

108
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:00,700
was the company that builds

109
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:02,050
those chip machines.

110
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:04,020
That's what connected looks like.

111
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:06,650
So, the money: halfway there.

112
00:03:07,830 --> 00:03:09,270
But why does it matter where

113
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:11,050
a company like that lives?

114
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:13,250
Arthur Mensch runs Mistral.

115
00:03:13,380 --> 00:03:15,150
He sells the European alternative,

116
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:17,080
so he has a stake in this.

117
00:03:17,320 --> 00:03:18,480
Here's his warning:

118
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:20,500
And if you can't afford

119
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:21,990
the business continuity,

120
00:03:22,030 --> 00:03:23,980
well, you're going to depend on,

121
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:24,910
basically you're becoming

122
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:26,680
a client state to the one

123
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:27,430
that is providing you

124
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:28,470
with the technology.

125
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:30,210
A client state.

126
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:31,520
He means the systems

127
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:33,540
a country cannot run without:

128
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:36,050
its defence, its public services.

129
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,420
And a client doesn't get a say.

130
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:40,220
So Europe needs companies

131
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:42,050
that can grow up at home.

132
00:03:42,260 --> 00:03:43,980
Money alone won't do it.

133
00:03:45,130 --> 00:03:45,870
Because a company

134
00:03:45,910 --> 00:03:47,490
that wants to grow across Europe

135
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:49,000
runs into 27

136
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:50,460
different company laws.

137
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:51,990
The plan has a fix:

138
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:53,390
a new kind of company,

139
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:55,680
valid in all 27 countries.

140
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,270
Set up online in 2 days,

141
00:03:57,310 --> 00:03:58,960
for under 100 euros.

142
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:00,030
But it's held up

143
00:04:00,070 --> 00:04:01,520
in the European Parliament.

144
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,430
Unions fear companies could use it

145
00:04:03,470 --> 00:04:05,110
to get around workers' rights.

146
00:04:05,150 --> 00:04:06,150
A fair worry.

147
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:07,640
And even if it's solved,

148
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:08,760
the first such company

149
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,200
isn't expected before 2028.

150
00:04:11,250 --> 00:04:12,830
So that one set of rules

151
00:04:12,870 --> 00:04:14,020
is still on paper.

152
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:15,610
None of it

153
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:17,410
runs without electricity.

154
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:18,680
AI data centres

155
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:20,510
need enormous amounts.

156
00:04:20,710 --> 00:04:21,850
And for heavy industry,

157
00:04:21,890 --> 00:04:23,730
electricity in the European Union

158
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:25,010
costs more than twice

159
00:04:25,050 --> 00:04:26,450
as much as in America.

160
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:27,880
But not everywhere.

161
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:29,070
Sweden's north has

162
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:30,390
cheap hydropower.

163
00:04:30,490 --> 00:04:32,130
France has its reactors.

164
00:04:32,370 --> 00:04:34,470
So the idea: build the biggest

165
00:04:34,510 --> 00:04:36,600
data centres where the power is.

166
00:04:36,730 --> 00:04:38,370
Europe wants up to 7.

167
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:39,470
The deadline for bids

168
00:04:39,510 --> 00:04:41,580
on the biggest: December 3.

169
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:43,060
But much of the public money

170
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:43,660
has to come

171
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:45,520
from Europe's next budget.

172
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:48,410
And even if all of them get built,

173
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:50,210
one Brussels think tank says,

174
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:53,110
Europe's 5% barely moves.

175
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:53,930
So this part

176
00:04:53,970 --> 00:04:55,450
is halfway there, too.

177
00:04:56,220 --> 00:04:57,740
One thing is settled:

178
00:04:57,970 --> 00:04:58,980
Europe won't have

179
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:00,290
the most computers.

180
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:01,540
It doesn't have to.

181
00:05:01,680 --> 00:05:03,200
It has to be needed.

182
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:04,670
And it already builds

183
00:05:04,710 --> 00:05:05,950
the one machine the world's

184
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:08,110
most advanced chips depend on.

185
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:10,200
This channel exists

186
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,560
for one reason:

187
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:12,560
so that the arrival

188
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:13,680
of superintelligence

189
00:05:13,720 --> 00:05:15,430
goes well for everybody.

190
00:05:15,620 --> 00:05:16,650
Wherever you live.

191
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Want that too?

192
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,130
Then subscribe.

193
00:05:20,310 --> 00:05:21,640
Europe does need enough

194
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:23,130
computers of its own.

195
00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:24,580
Enough for what?

196
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:26,400
For one shared lab,

197
00:05:26,440 --> 00:05:28,540
building the most advanced AI.

198
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:30,360
Europe has done this before.

199
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:31,310
After the war,

200
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:32,380
its countries pooled

201
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,440
their money for one physics lab.

202
00:05:34,680 --> 00:05:35,550
The World Wide Web

203
00:05:35,590 --> 00:05:36,720
was invented there.

204
00:05:36,770 --> 00:05:38,050
It's called CERN.

205
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:39,630
And Europe's top official,

206
00:05:39,670 --> 00:05:40,980
Ursula von der Leyen,

207
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,100
has promised a CERN for AI.

208
00:05:43,410 --> 00:05:45,450
So far, that promise is a pilot

209
00:05:45,490 --> 00:05:47,650
worth about 100 million euros.

210
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:49,100
In November, she named

211
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:51,140
the price of really catching up:

212
00:05:51,980 --> 00:05:52,480
All this,

213
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:54,050
what I've just described,

214
00:05:54,090 --> 00:05:56,820
needs massive investment.

215
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:58,720
At least 500 billion euros,

216
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:02,010
according to our own estimates.

217
00:06:02,510 --> 00:06:05,320
At least 500 billion euros.

218
00:06:05,490 --> 00:06:06,490
Her estimate.

219
00:06:06,610 --> 00:06:07,610
Not a budget.

220
00:06:07,920 --> 00:06:09,630
A name and a pilot.

221
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:12,110
The lab, too, is still on paper.

222
00:06:13,270 --> 00:06:15,590
And a lab is an empty building

223
00:06:15,630 --> 00:06:17,230
without the fifth part.

224
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:18,960
The one that can't be built:

225
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:20,280
minds.

226
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:22,620
Picture a researcher in Paris,

227
00:06:22,660 --> 00:06:24,130
just finished her doctorate.

228
00:06:24,390 --> 00:06:25,390
Two offers.

229
00:06:25,480 --> 00:06:27,000
One keeps her in Europe.

230
00:06:27,110 --> 00:06:28,590
The other, in America,

231
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:30,770
can pay up to 70% more.

232
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:32,350
Before stock options.

233
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:33,620
Many take it.

234
00:06:33,930 --> 00:06:35,290
Part of Europe's answer:

235
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:36,550
stock options that work

236
00:06:36,590 --> 00:06:38,270
the same in every country,

237
00:06:38,310 --> 00:06:40,050
taxed only when you sell.

238
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:42,330
But look where that answer sits.

239
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:43,470
Inside that new

240
00:06:43,510 --> 00:06:44,910
Europe-wide company.

241
00:06:45,090 --> 00:06:46,610
The one that's still on paper.

242
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:49,510
And yet, something has shifted.

243
00:06:49,830 --> 00:06:50,830
Researchers leaving

244
00:06:50,870 --> 00:06:52,640
the European Union for America

245
00:06:52,680 --> 00:06:54,360
are down by almost half

246
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,410
in 2 years.

247
00:06:55,820 --> 00:06:56,670
A few are even

248
00:06:56,710 --> 00:06:57,840
moving the other way.

249
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,320
So the fifth part stays open.

250
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:02,440
It can't be passed in Brussels.

251
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:03,860
It has to be won,

252
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,730
one person at a time.

253
00:07:06,900 --> 00:07:09,860
So suppose all five come together.

254
00:07:10,110 --> 00:07:11,420
What does Europe win?

255
00:07:11,800 --> 00:07:13,420
Not the biggest chatbot.

256
00:07:13,550 --> 00:07:15,480
But a second race is still open:

257
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,350
putting AI to work.

258
00:07:17,630 --> 00:07:19,340
One satellite maker is using it

259
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:21,040
to cut its development time.

260
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:22,800
And more than half of Europe's

261
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:23,720
biggest companies

262
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:25,640
are over 100 years old.

263
00:07:25,840 --> 00:07:26,800
Much of what they know

264
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:28,630
has never been on the internet.

265
00:07:28,690 --> 00:07:30,390
No chatbot has read it.

266
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:31,740
That argument comes

267
00:07:31,780 --> 00:07:33,070
from Peter Koerte,

268
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:34,950
a senior Siemens executive.

269
00:07:35,300 --> 00:07:36,990
Siemens sells this kind of AI,

270
00:07:37,030 --> 00:07:38,660
so he isn't neutral.

271
00:07:38,830 --> 00:07:40,180
But listen to his point:

272
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:41,520
And that's the trick,

273
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:43,460
because none of us

274
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:45,840
can do it alone.

275
00:07:45,880 --> 00:07:46,890
Siemens can't.

276
00:07:46,930 --> 00:07:47,980
Yes, we do produce.

277
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:49,050
We produce trains,

278
00:07:49,090 --> 00:07:50,760
substations and so on.

279
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,260
But we don't have enough data.

280
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:54,010
OHB doesn't have enough data,

281
00:07:54,050 --> 00:07:55,060
and also KION doesn't have

282
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:56,100
enough data.

283
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:58,760
No single company has enough data.

284
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:00,750
Together, they could.

285
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:02,060
The other prize

286
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:03,890
is a say in the rules.

287
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:06,020
A say goes to whoever builds

288
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:08,100
what others can't do without.

289
00:08:08,210 --> 00:08:09,790
Like those chip machines.

290
00:08:09,940 --> 00:08:11,650
But it only counts if Europe

291
00:08:11,690 --> 00:08:13,390
speaks with one voice.

292
00:08:13,580 --> 00:08:15,120
Today, the Dutch government

293
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:17,080
decides who gets the machines,

294
00:08:17,150 --> 00:08:18,550
Washington has a say,

295
00:08:18,690 --> 00:08:20,560
and China is building its own.

296
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,360
How to get to one voice

297
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:23,920
is an open argument:

298
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:26,640
more shared money, or fewer rules.

299
00:08:27,220 --> 00:08:28,300
Who settles it?

300
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:29,410
Governments.

301
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:31,050
The European Parliament.

302
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:32,150
Investors.

303
00:08:32,310 --> 00:08:34,890
And at the next election, voters.

304
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:38,360
So that's where the plan stands:

305
00:08:38,530 --> 00:08:40,200
two parts halfway there,

306
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:43,030
two on paper, one still to be won.

307
00:08:43,230 --> 00:08:44,640
None is finished.

308
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:46,820
But every one of them has started.

309
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:48,500
Can Europe still win?

310
00:08:48,770 --> 00:08:50,610
The race for the most computers:

311
00:08:50,710 --> 00:08:51,450
no.

312
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:54,090
The race to put AI to work: yes.

313
00:08:54,250 --> 00:08:56,570
That one, Europe can still win.

314
00:08:56,640 --> 00:08:58,570
And with it, a voice of its own.

315
00:08:58,660 --> 00:08:59,970
A say in which AI

316
00:09:00,010 --> 00:09:01,760
sits in on your job interview.

317
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:02,930
Mario Draghi,

318
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:04,970
who wrote the plan, now says:

319
00:09:05,910 --> 00:09:08,360
Of all those now caught between

320
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:11,020
the United States and China,

321
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:13,980
Europeans alone have the option

322
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:16,530
to become a genuine power

323
00:09:16,570 --> 00:09:17,590
themselves.

324
00:09:18,110 --> 00:09:19,110
An option.

325
00:09:19,220 --> 00:09:20,390
With a clock on it.

326
00:09:21,630 --> 00:09:23,470
This winter, Europe's leaders

327
00:09:23,510 --> 00:09:25,080
are negotiating the budget

328
00:09:25,120 --> 00:09:26,960
that much of this depends on.

329
00:09:27,230 --> 00:09:29,070
Europe builds the machines.

330
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:30,510
It has the savings.

331
00:09:30,590 --> 00:09:32,270
It trains the minds.

332
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:33,970
Europe was never too small

333
00:09:34,010 --> 00:09:35,070
for this race.

334
00:09:35,230 --> 00:09:36,620
The only question is whether

335
00:09:36,660 --> 00:09:38,890
it puts itself together in time.

336
00:09:40,050 --> 00:09:41,120
This channel exists

337
00:09:41,160 --> 00:09:42,500
for one reason:

338
00:09:42,650 --> 00:09:43,530
so that the arrival

339
00:09:43,570 --> 00:09:44,680
of superintelligence

340
00:09:44,720 --> 00:09:46,540
goes well for everybody.

341
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:47,910
That takes more voices

342
00:09:47,950 --> 00:09:49,620
at the table, not fewer.

343
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:50,780
Want that too?

344
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:51,910
Then subscribe.

